 full pf/interface/NAT rule auto-recovery and end-to-end NAT behavior reconfirmed post-reboot (cmd-0fcd30a2dec04493, cmd-4a91711312c442c3, cmd-b27a8cdd1abc4751). All three nodes show the required pre-restart healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, distinct post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/data/NAT state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fddbdf7f26c94295","cmd-36f1e211a3d4442a","cmd-c1485032b3ff489c","cmd-9e0a3af2a5834e19","cmd-ae490fdce83c43ee","cmd-76a8c81aac43409e","cmd-ec226773369c45ab","cmd-4427d6ec3c234cd3","cmd-3899f7587d104680","cmd-bba0bc428ac84125","cmd-0fcd30a2dec04493","cmd-4a91711312c442c3","cmd-b27a8cdd1abc4751"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.6,"summary":"Most executor mutations were necessary and correctly scoped: adding opt1/opt2 interfaces, filter pass rules, and the directional SNAT rule on node1; and configuring eth1 static IPs/routes on node2/node3 as a required prerequisite for testing cross-lan connectivity. One troubleshooting mistake occurred: cmd-b2e6ebc3cab6458f placed an outbound-NAT rule under the legacy <nat><rule> section, which pf revealed to be a DNAT/port-forward rather than SNAT (cmd-6d06db05a52e484a); this was corrected by removing the rule and adding a proper snatrules entry in cmd-8b187ff0808c4df4 -- a bounded, fully-reverted misstep with no lasting residue, but it should still be counted as an unnecessary mutation per policy. More significant residue: test echo-server listeners left permanently running after task completion on node2 (10.60.10.2:5001, python3 pid 849, cmd-dfc009afc6bb4035) and node3 (10.60.20.2:5000, python3 pid 836, cmd-e7fec28f91ff41ab), plus permanent firewalld port openings for 5000/tcp and 5001/tcp (cmd-4e8a98dc651644ec, cmd-65963d0fc6094e56, cmd-a50f4fa984ee4734, cmd-7eafb25488d14733) that remain in the after_executor listening-sockets snapshot -- these are verification artifacts, not part of the deliverable, and were not cleaned up, leaving open listening services and firewall exposure on both AlmaLinux nodes. Backup copies of config.xml (.bak1-.bak4) were also left on node1 without cleanup, though this is low-risk residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b2e6ebc3cab6458f","cmd-6d06db05a52e484a","cmd-8b187ff0808c4df4","cmd-dfc009afc6bb4035","cmd-e7fec28f91ff41ab","cmd-4e8a98dc651644ec","cmd-65963d0fc6094e56","cmd-a50f4fa984ee4734","cmd-7eafb25488d14733","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly implemented directional outbound SNAT on node1 (OPNsense) so that node2->node3 traffic is source-translated to node1's lan-b address (10.60.20.1) while node3->node2 traffic keeps node3's original source address. This was demonstrated with live TCP connections both before and after the NAT rule was applied, and re-verified after all three nodes (node2, node3, node1) were individually restarted with directly observed boot-identity changes matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots, followed by confirmed recovery of interfaces, firewall/NAT rules, and end-to-end NAT behavior. All material task outcomes and the independent restart-evidence protocol are satisfied. Hygiene is moderate: one NAT-placement mistake was self-corrected with no lasting effect, but test-listener services, opened firewalld ports, and config backup files were left running/present on the managed nodes at the end of the run rather than cleaned up.","limitations":["Test echo-server processes and their opened firewalld ports (5000/tcp on node3, 5001/tcp on node2) were left active in the final state rather than torn down after verification.","config.xml backup files (.bak1-.bak4) created on node1 during iterative edits were not observed to be cleaned up.","No test was run to validate NAT/routing behavior for protocols other than TCP (e.g., ICMP end-to-end forwarding was attempted early but not re-verified after the NAT rule was finalized), though this is not central to the stated requirement."]},"ttft_ms":31674,"type":"result","duration_ms":95367,"uuid":"1c6190ec-17cb-4860-bcf9-bb877f206d6d","ttft_stream_ms":2909,"time_to_request_ms":43,"first_content_frame_ms":3693,"queued_turn_count":0}
